---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ColorConverter from '@/components/tools/dev/ColorConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="color-converter">
  <ColorConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a colour</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type or paste a value in any supported notation, or use the swatch picker.</li>
      <li>The table below shows all other formats immediately, each with its own copy button.</li>
      <li>Drag the RGB or HSL sliders to try nearby colours. The text field stays in sync.</li>
      <li>Set a background colour to get the WCAG contrast ratio and pass or fail badges for each level.</li>
      <li>Click a tint or shade to make it the new working colour.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What each colour space is for</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Space</th><th>Model</th><th>Best used for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>HEX</td><td>Three or four bytes of sRGB</td><td>Design handoff, CSS, anywhere compactness matters</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RGB</td><td>Additive red, green, blue</td><td>What screens emit; canvas and image pixel work</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HSL</td><td>Hue, saturation, lightness</td><td>Quick manual changes to hue or lightness</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HSV / HSB</td><td>Hue, saturation, value</td><td>The model behind most colour pickers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OKLCH</td><td>Perceptual lightness, chroma, hue</td><td>Generating palettes and states that look evenly spaced</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CMYK</td><td>Subtractive cyan, magenta, yellow, key</td><td>Print, but only as an approximation without an ICC profile</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why OKLCH beats HSL for palettes</h2>
    <p>
      HSL "lightness" is the average of the highest and lowest RGB channels, and it does not match how
      the eye works. Pure yellow at <code>hsl(60 100% 50%)</code> and pure blue at
      <code>hsl(240 100% 50%)</code> have the same HSL lightness, but the yellow is very bright and the
      blue is nearly black. If you make a set of buttons by rotating the hue at a fixed HSL lightness,
      some need white text and others need black.
    </p>
    <p>
      OKLCH uses the Oklab model, which was fitted to perceptual data. Equal changes on its L axis look
      like equal changes in brightness for all hues, so a scale made by stepping L gives a consistent
      palette. For this reason CSS Color 4 added it, and design systems are moving to it.
    </p>

    <h2>How WCAG contrast is calculated</h2>
    <p>
      Each sRGB channel is divided by 255, then linearised: values at or below 0.04045 are divided by
      12.92, and the rest go through <code>((c + 0.055) / 1.055)<sup>2.4</sup></code>. Relative
      luminance is then <code>0.2126R + 0.7152G + 0.0722B</code>. Green has the largest weight because
      human vision is most sensitive to it. The ratio between two colours is
      <code>(L<sub>lighter</sub> + 0.05) / (L<sub>darker</sub> + 0.05)</code>, a number between 1:1 and
      21:1.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Level AA</th><th>Level AAA</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Body text under 18pt (24px)</td><td>4.5:1</td><td>7:1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large text: 18pt (24px), or 14pt (18.66px) bold</td><td>3:1</td><td>4.5:1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Icons, borders and other meaningful UI</td><td>3:1</td><td>Not defined</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decorative graphics, logos, disabled controls</td><td>Exempt</td><td>Exempt</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The ratio is a minimum, not proof of legibility. A thin font weight at 4.5:1 is still hard to read,
      and the formula ignores font size, weight and layout. Check the sample block above as well as the
      number.
    </p>

    <h2>Transparency</h2>
    <p>
      Contrast is defined only between two opaque colours, so the tool first flattens a translucent
      foreground onto the background you chose, as the browser does. When you change the background,
      the ratio changes: a colour at 60% alpha that passes on white can fail on a mid-grey card.
    </p>

    <h2>About the CMYK figures</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion uses the standard device-independent formula with no colour management. Real print
      output depends on the press, the ink set, the paper and an ICC profile, and many sRGB colours
      (saturated cyans and greens most of all) cannot be printed in CMYK. Use these numbers as a
      starting point to discuss with your printer, not as final artwork values.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
